\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The odd Wick rule pairs inverse alternating entries}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{A^T=-A} be an invertible #{2m\times2m} matrix.
Its [nonzero Pfaffian](fdip-008J) defines the normalized
linear functional
##{W_A(f)=\frac{I_\xi(f e^{-\xi^TA\xi/2})}
                 {\operatorname{Pf}(A)}.}
For linear Grassmann elements
#{\lambda_a=\sum_i\ell_{ai}\xi_i}, put
#{C_{ab}=\ell_aA^{-1}\ell_b^T}. For every #{r\ge0},
##{W_A(\lambda_1\cdots\lambda_{2r})=\operatorname{Pf}(C),}
where #{C} is the #{2r\times2r} alternating matrix just defined.
Every odd product has value zero. Repeated or dependent
#{\lambda_a} are allowed. The inverse formula requires
invertible #{A}; it asserts no singular extension.}

\proof{
\p{Define the left derivative on a monomial by
##{\partial_i(\xi_{j_1}\cdots\xi_{j_s})
 =\sum_{a=1}^s(-1)^{a-1}\delta_{i,j_a}
   \xi_{j_1}\cdots\widehat{\xi_{j_a}}\cdots\xi_{j_s}.}
This respects the exterior relations. Splitting the removed
positions between two factors gives the graded product rule
##{\partial_i(fg)=(\partial_i f)g+(-1)^s f(\partial_i g)}
when #{f} is homogeneous of degree #{s}. It lowers degree,
so #{I_\xi(\partial_i h)=0} for every #{h}.}

\p{Write #{E=e^{-\xi^TA\xi/2}}. Differentiating its finite
series, using that its quadratic exponent is even, gives
##{\partial_iE=-\sum_jA_{ij}\xi_jE.}
For odd homogeneous #{f}, the product rule and coefficient of
the total derivative imply
##{\begin{aligned}
0&=I_\xi((\partial_i f)E+f\sum_jA_{ij}\xi_jE),\\
\sum_jA_{ij}W_A(\xi_jf)&=W_A(\partial_i f).
\end{aligned}}
The second line uses #{f\xi_j=-\xi_jf}. Multiplying by
#{A^{-1}} gives
##{W_A(\xi_kf)=\sum_i(A^{-1})_{ki}W_A(\partial_i f).}
}

\p{Apply this to #{f=\lambda_2\cdots\lambda_{2r}} and
expand #{\lambda_1}. The derivative removes its #{a}-th
listed factor with sign #{(-1)^{a-2}}, yielding
##{\begin{aligned}
W_A(\lambda_1\cdots\lambda_{2r})
 &=\sum_{a=2}^{2r}(-1)^a C_{1a}\\
 &\quad\cdot W_A(\lambda_2\cdots\widehat{\lambda_a}
                         \cdots\lambda_{2r}).
\end{aligned}}
Starting from #{W_A(1)=1}, this is exactly the
[Pfaffian recursion](fdip-008I). Odd products have odd degree,
while #{E} has only even degrees and the selected top degree
#{2m} is even; hence their integrals vanish.}
}

\p{For #{A=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&a\\-a&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)}
with #{a\ne0}, the [two-generator calculation](fdip-008G) gives
#{W_A(\xi_1\xi_2)=-1/a=(A^{-1})_{12}}. This checks the
inverse-pairing sign. In
\citet{v2, Theorem 9.11, p. 132}{etingof2024mathematical},
the increasing top order gives normalization #{\operatorname{Pf}(-A)}.
Changing the volume multiplies both numerator and normalization
by the same sign, leaving this normalized Wick rule unchanged.}
